## secure-chat onion service (added 2026-07-28)
##
## This daemon exists to publish ONE onion service. It is not a client proxy
## and not a relay, so everything that would make it either is switched off.
## Overrides the package defaults in
## /usr/share/tor/tor-service-defaults-torrc (torrc wins over that file).
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# No SOCKS. The package default opens 127.0.0.1:9050 AND a WORLD-WRITABLE unix
# socket at /run/tor/socks; neither is needed to publish a service, and both
# would let any local process route traffic through Tor. 0 disables both.
SOCKSPort 0

# Never act as a relay, directory mirror, or exit.
ORPort 0
DirPort 0
ExitRelay 0

# Metadata minimization (I2). The relay itself writes no access log; Tor must
# not reintroduce one. SafeLogging scrubs addresses, and warn drops the routine
# notice-level chatter so a seized host yields no who-connected-when trail.
SafeLogging 1
Log warn syslog

# The service. Tor terminates the onion protocol and forwards plaintext HTTP to
# the loopback uvicorn — the same socket Caddy proxies to, so both front ends
# share one process (no split-brain rooms). uvicorn itself stays bound to
# 127.0.0.1 and is never exposed on a public interface.
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/secure-chat/
HiddenServiceVersion 3
HiddenServicePort 80 127.0.0.1:8000

# Proof-of-work defence against introduction floods (tor >= 0.4.8). This
# matters more here than on a typical onion: SECURE_CHAT_TRUSTED_PROXIES is
# deliberately unset, so every /api rate limiter collapses to ONE global
# bucket, and the cheapest attack on the service is simply volume.
HiddenServicePoWDefensesEnabled 1
